WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.800
What I want to do in this video is give ourselves a quick overview of the different ways that cells can communicate with each other.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.900
And maybe the most basic one is just through...

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.300
direct contact.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.804
direct contact, and this can happen several different ways.

00:00:14.804 --> 00:00:17.884
One way could be you could just have neighboring cells.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:19.900
So this is one cell right over here.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.400
and this is the neighboring cell right over here.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.000
and they actually might

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.501
have gaps in their membranes. If these are plant cells,

00:00:29.501 --> 00:00:31.700
we would call these gaps gap junctions.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.215
So you might have,

00:00:33.215 --> 00:00:39.869
I just drew two of them and this is obviously just kind of a two dimensional slice so we're not fully appreciating the three dimensional structure.

00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:43.400
But the basic idea here is maybe the cell on the left starts producing some...

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.400
molecules, especially some small molecules that are able to go through these gap junctions.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.368
And when they're able to go through the gap junctions,

00:00:51.368 --> 00:00:59.865
maybe they latch on to some proteins in the second cell that start maybe activating them in a certain way or inhibit them in a certain way or trigger some type of reaction.

00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:01.100
And so then you have a...

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.200
Response.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.627
in the second cell. So this would be a form of communication.

00:01:05.627 --> 00:01:08.640
Something happened in this first cell to produce these molecules.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.209
Those molecules were able to get into the second cell and trigger a response.

00:01:12.209 --> 00:01:15.500
And as I mentioned, if these are animal cells, we would call these gaps

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.600
call them gap junctions.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.500
Right over there, that is a gap junction.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.100
junction

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.042
Now we covered this in other videos. And if we're talking about plant cells,

00:01:28.042 --> 00:01:31.582
we can have very similar things happening, but we wouldn't call them gap junctions.

00:01:31.582 --> 00:01:34.994
These would be gaps not just through the, not even tunnels through the membrane,

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.700
it would also be through the cell walls.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.700
If they were talking about plants, they would be plasmodesmata

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.600
Plasmodesmata, and we talk about these more in the structure of cell videos.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.800
But there's other ways that you could communicate via direct contact.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.700
You can imagine maybe some cells that are floating around in the bloodstream.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.300
Maybe this is one cell over here.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.100
Maybe.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.800
Maybe this is another cell over here.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.581
and they have complementary surface proteins,

00:02:04.581 --> 00:02:07.150
surface proteins that are able to bind to each other.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.107
So maybe this one has a surface protein that looks like this.

00:02:10.107 --> 00:02:11.900
I'm obviously exaggerating its shape.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.900
protein would look exactly like this inverted triangle.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.691
but this one has a surface protein like this,

00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:21.100
and I'm also exaggerating its size relative to a cell just so we can--

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.900
appreciate how they could maybe lock together.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.495
So this one has a surface protein like that. And when they bind to each other,

00:02:28.495 --> 00:02:34.395
it might change the proteins in some way and then trigger reaction in each of these cells that the communication then continues.

00:02:34.395 --> 00:02:37.437
If this protein changes a little bit, it might activate something,

00:02:37.437 --> 00:02:41.309
it might activate the release of some molecules, might activate some other proteins,

00:02:41.309 --> 00:02:42.000
might catalyze.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.584
some type of reaction. We've seen this in multiple other videos.

00:02:45.584 --> 00:02:49.232
All sorts of crazy biological reactions could happen inside of cells,

00:02:49.232 --> 00:02:51.400
but it might elicit a, it could elicit...

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.600
a response and actually could even elicit a response in both cells.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.500
some type of response. They know that they are latched on to someone else.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.121
Now, direct contact, you could imagine,

00:03:04.121 --> 00:03:07.077
is not the only way that you could have cell-cell signaling.

00:03:07.077 --> 00:03:15.500
You could actually have cells start to signal over a little bit of a distance by releasing molecules into the extracellular space and eventually even into the bloodstream.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:16.900
So...

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.100
For example,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.500
Let me draw

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:20.200
cell

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.700
So all right over here

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.900
and maybe it's able to produce some molecules.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.488
and these molecules,

00:03:28.488 --> 00:03:34.603
either they might be lipid soluble and maybe make their way through the cellular membrane on their own,

00:03:34.603 --> 00:03:34.900
or...

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.300
Maybe they are.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.100
packed in nice vesicles that allow them

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.300
to traverse the membrane, so when you get actually...

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.700
So the membrane of the vesicle merges with the--

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.400
membrane of the cell.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:49.900
and then it allows

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.000
these things.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:51.900
to

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.000
get out.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.720
And so you can imagine if another cell has the right receptors for these,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.100
that would signal some type of a response or it will form some form of communication.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.100
Let's say that I have, actually let me draw a couple of cells.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.200
So let's have this cell over here.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:10.700
and then I have

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.600
this cell over here.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:15.100
and

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.900
Let's say that this cell has the right receptor.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.000
as the right receptor

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.900
And this cell doesn't. It could have other receptors. Maybe it has receptors like--

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:25.900
that

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.775
but it's not the right receptor.

00:04:27.775 --> 00:04:32.106
And so these molecules that were released by this first cell could bind not on this character,

00:04:32.106 --> 00:04:35.700
not on this protein, the surface protein, but it could bind on this character.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.000
So it combined on this character.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.700
And when it does so

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.825
protein that's on the cellular membrane, it might change its shape,

00:04:44.825 --> 00:04:50.900
it might do all sorts of things, but that signal can be then taken somehow, it can be...

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.900
It can continue on into the cell and once again...

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.300
you might elicit some type of response.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.600
And we'll go into more detail in future videos on exactly how that happens or what these responses actually might be.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.300
Now, if this is over a short distance--

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.900
if this is over, if this is a short distance.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:11.300
Short.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.300
This is a short distance.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:16.400
Short.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:18.000
distance.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:21.400
We would call this paracrine.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.500
This would be part of the paracrine system, or we would call this paracrine communication.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:29.300
paracrine

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:31.800
Paracrine

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:33.200
system.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.100
Paracrine

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:39.823
communication or paracrine signaling, and we would call these paracrine factors.

00:05:39.823 --> 00:05:42.200
But if it was happening over long distances,

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:46.200
So maybe these molecules, they enter into the bloodstream.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.400
So they make their way into the bloodstream right over here.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.500
I will pick some color for the bloodstream.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:53.700
This is

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:55.600
bloodstream.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.000
bloodstream.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.437
and they're able to make,

00:05:59.437 --> 00:06:02.300
and they're able to go through the bloodstream over longer distances.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.100
to other cells

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.100
to other cells, so maybe this one has

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.700
the right receptors,

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:09.900
for those.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:11.500
for those.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:13.900
for those molecules.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.300
Then we call this

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.000
endocrine system, or this is endocrine signaling.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.900
So long distances

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.000
Long.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.700
Long distances.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.000
we would call this the endocrine system or endocrine.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.900
endocrine signaling.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:31.800
crin.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.200
And we're talking about the endocrine system and endocrine signaling.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.063
these molecules, which could just be, you know,

00:06:39.063 --> 00:06:42.963
there could be all sorts of different types of molecules. They could be steroids,

00:06:42.963 --> 00:06:47.200
they could be proteins of some kind. We call, in this case, we would call them hormones.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.000
heard the word before and we will do a whole series of videos on hormones.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:56.100
but these molecules actually can even affect the cell that produced them.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.800
For example, the cell that produced it might have the right receptor.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.100
might have the right receptor.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.642
And so if it's able to trigger a reaction in itself,

00:07:05.642 --> 00:07:09.267
so if these things are able to trigger a reaction in itself,

00:07:09.267 --> 00:07:11.200
we would call that an autocrine.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.200
process.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:13.400
auto

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.900
It is acting on itself.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.600
and just so you're familiar with some of the terminology.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.523
these proteins on the surface.

00:07:21.523 --> 00:07:30.297
And this would be the case especially if you have non lipid soluble types of signaling factors or molecules right over here.

00:07:30.297 --> 00:07:31.500
These proteins...

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.200
Well, actually, even if they're on the surface or even if they're within the cell, we

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.100
So that right over there, that is a receptor.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.438
And the molecules themselves, these things that bind onto the receptor,

00:07:45.438 --> 00:07:46.600
we call them the ligand.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:52.200
ligand. In general, it's a general term for something that binds onto a receptor.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:57.027
And so at that point where you bind onto a receptor,

00:07:57.027 --> 00:08:04.100
whether you're talking about the paracrine process or endocrine, endocrine signaling.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.635
this process where it kind of starts to, where it latches on,

00:08:08.635 --> 00:08:10.900
we would call that a signal reception.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:18.100
And then when this protein somehow changes its shape or starts catalyzing a reaction or inhibiting a reaction.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.000
We would call this the signal transduction.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:22.800
trans

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:23.800
duction.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.300
It's bringing the signal into the cell. And then you have your actual--

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.900
you have your actual cellular response.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:39.719
So hopefully you appreciate that as just a bit of an overview of how cells can signal with each other.

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:42.694
In future videos we'll go into a little bit more detail,

00:08:42.694 --> 00:08:46.200
especially the endocrine system and our understanding of hormones.
